CoreFoundation on Linux with no Swift above it: _CFIsSwift() is always false

Applies to swift-corelibs-foundation (Sources/CoreFoundation), not to dyld.

swift-corelibs-foundation 6.x has no "plain C CoreFoundation" configuration:
include/CFAvailability.h:11 and internalInclude/CoreFoundation_Prefix.h:11
both #define DEPLOYMENT_RUNTIME_SWIFT 1 unconditionally.  Almost all of what
that costs a C-only client is satisfied by the stub symbols in
base/darwin/toolchain/shims/swift-corelibs-foundation/cf_swift_stubs.c -- but not this one.

_CFIsSwift(type, obj) answers "did Swift Foundation make this object?" with
`obj->isa != __CFRuntimeObjCClassTable[type]`.  That table is filled in by
__CFInitializeSwift(), which is a Swift Foundation entry point and is a no-op
stub here, so every entry is NULL.  CF's own heap objects come out of
swift_allocObject() with a NULL metadata pointer and so compare equal (not
Swift, correct), and constant strings are special-cased on the line above --
but the *statically initialised* constants are not: kCFBooleanTrue,
kCFBooleanFalse and the constant CFNumbers carry a real class pointer in their
isa, so they test as Swift objects and CFGetTypeID() dispatches through
__CFSwiftBridge.NSObject._cfTypeID, which is NULL.

Symptom without this patch: SIGSEGV at address 0 inside CFPropertyList's
validation of the __PRELINK_INFO dictionary, the first time it reaches a
boolean --

    #0  0x0000000000000000 in ?? ()
    #1  __CFPropertyListIsValidAux ()
    #2  __CFPropertyListIsDictPlistAux ()
    ...
    #7  CFPropertyListCreateData ()
    #8  AppCacheBuilder::assignSegmentRegionsAndOffsets ()

There is no Swift runtime in this process and no Swift object can reach CF, so
the honest answer is a constant false.

diff --git a/Sources/CoreFoundation/CFRuntime.c b/Sources/CoreFoundation/CFRuntime.c
index 3336852..4153df6 100644
--- a/Sources/CoreFoundation/CFRuntime.c
+++ b/Sources/CoreFoundation/CFRuntime.c
@@ -1810,11 +1810,15 @@ CF_CROSS_PLATFORM_EXPORT void _CFDeinit(CFTypeRef cf) {
 }
 
 bool _CFIsSwift(CFTypeID type, CFSwiftRef obj) {
-    if (type == _kCFRuntimeNotATypeID) {
-        return false;
-    }
-    if (obj->isa == (uintptr_t)__CFConstantStringClassReferencePtr) return false;
-    return obj->isa != __CFRuntimeObjCClassTable[type];
+    // base/darwin/toolchain/build-dyld-app-cache-util.sh (Darwin-bench): this CoreFoundation is linked into a
+    // pure C program with no Swift Foundation above it, so no object here is
+    // ever a Swift one.  The stock test is "isa differs from the Swift class
+    // registered for this type", and __CFRuntimeObjCClassTable is all NULL
+    // because __CFInitializeSwift() never registers anything -- which makes
+    // every constant object (kCFBooleanTrue, the constant CFNumbers) look
+    // Swift and dispatch through a NULL __CFSwiftBridge entry.
+    (void)type; (void)obj;
+    return false;
 }
 
 const char *_NSPrintForDebugger(void *cf) {
